WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.130
- [Narrator] This video
is taken near the eye

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:06.000
of a category four hurricane
in the middle of the Atlantic

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.280
with waves as high as
a four-story building

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.340
and winds reaching 94 miles an hour.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.130
The National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.740
is sending three state-of-the-art drones

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.990
each with its own important mission

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.611
straight into the center
of these monster storms.

00:00:22.611 --> 00:00:23.444
(wind blowing)

00:00:23.444 --> 00:00:25.350
- The drones are a new piece of technology

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.396
that is helping us uncover

00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:31.530
information about the storms
that we didn't know before.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.390
- [Narrator] Let's take
a look inside NOAA's lab

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.620
to see how the agency uses
drones to dive, sail and fly

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.833
into parts of a hurricane
where humans could never go.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.450
At the laboratory, a
team of NOAA engineers

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.900
work on this torpedo-shaped
drone called the Glider.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.470
It's five feet long, can
weigh up to 150 pounds,

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:56.100
and it's controlled by drone
pilots like Ulises Rivero.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.900
- A Glider could cost anywhere
from 175,000 to $200,000

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.100
depending on what sensors
they're equipped with.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.440
- Those sensors are used to track storms

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.660
half a mile beneath the ocean surface

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.510
measuring water
temperatures along the way.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.300
- Ooh, beautiful.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.133
- Better.

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.400
- This is not a handle.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.770
This is where you have
the conductivity depth

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.240
and temperature sensor in here,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.890
and this is a dissolved oxygen.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:23.760
- [Narrator] The unit can find

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.680
the warmest sections of water,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.110
which can intensify hurricanes.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.722
- Normally, we dive 'em to 900 meters

00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:33.060
and it takes about four hours

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.520
so it's about six dives per day.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.380
- [Narrator] The drones
collect data underwater

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.300
and send it to computers like this

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.700
using satellite radio waves.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.440
- So, one of the things we want to know is

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.480
when the Glider goes into the water,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.630
if you look here, this is the actual track

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.740
of what the Glider did,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.870
and you see this is like
almost a perfect V-shape,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.940
which is what we strive for.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.860
- [Narrator] When the drones
surface after each dive,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.380
they transmit their location
to Ulises' computer,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.070
which shows up as those
little dots between the lines.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.630
- So here is a map of where
our Gliders are right now.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.970
So, we have Gliders south of Puerto Rico,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.250
these three Gliders right here,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.620
one south of the Dominican Republic,

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.200
and one north of Puerto Rico.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.120
- [Narrator] While Gliders
take measurements underwater,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.540
sail drones track what's
happening on the ocean surface.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.520
- They want to get the sail drone

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.830
to be at the same location as the Glider.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.710
This way you have information
from the surface down

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.440
and then also, you know,
atmospheric information,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.710
'cause the sail drones have
the capability of doing that.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.300
- [Narrator] Built for extreme condition,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.900
the robots are 23 feet
long with a 15-foot wing

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.100
that powers the drone,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.250
and solar panels that power the sensors.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.770
These sensors test
surface level temperature,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.890
wave height, wind speed, and salinity

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.232
or how fresh the water is.

00:02:52.232 --> 00:02:53.649
- And if they are fresh,

00:02:53.649 --> 00:02:56.417
we know that they will
remain in the surface.

00:02:56.417 --> 00:02:58.350
- [Narrator] And if
that water is also warm,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:02.070
it indicates a site that
could intensify a hurricane.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.350
So, one of Gustavo Goni's teams

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.530
marks those areas on a
map to keep an eye on.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:08.970
And when a hurricane is coming,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:12.258
the robots can sail
straight into the storm.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.490
- But still under those conditions

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:19.080
it has to conduct the
observations and transmit them.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.730
- [Narrator] 2022 was the first year

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.280
that NOAA deployed Gliders and sail drones

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.770
to the same areas of the Atlantic.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.570
And this season, NOAA is adding

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.940
super tough, new aerial drones

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.910
that can fly through the
200 mile per hour winds

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.040
around the eye of a hurricane.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.410
- These drones provide us that information

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.290
from the temperature,
the relative humidity,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.330
the pressure information, wind,

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.710
wind speed, and wind direction.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.690
All of those factors are key

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.560
on how we understand how
the hurricane is evolving.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.600
That data goes into the computer models

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.220
so that we can better forecast

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.860
what's gonna happen
the next day and so on.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.390
- [Narrator] Shirely Murillo is in charge

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.510
of the hurricane research
department at the lab.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.610
For years, hurricane hunter planes

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.583
have flown above hurricanes
to take measurements.

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:09.060
- Oh, there goes the signs.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.440
- [Narrator] Today, aerial drones

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.120
are launched from those planes

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.920
flying into the eye of the storm,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.020
giving experts a wealth of new data

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.320
about how these monster storms intensify.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.930
- Hurricane intensity
is a tough nut to crack.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.030
There are so many different
pieces of the puzzle

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.890
that need to be solved

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.950
from looking at the different
layers of the atmosphere,

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.930
from vertically to horizontally
to different timescales

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:34.763
- [Narrator] From the air,

00:04:34.763 --> 00:04:37.560
scientists at these
stations pilot the drones

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.740
gliding them like hawks
into gale force winds.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.403
Sometimes closer than 100
feet from the ocean surface.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.960
Last year, NOAA flew this
drone, the Altius 600,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.510
into the eye of a category four hurricane

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.370
for over two hours,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.000
but the drone crashed
and wasn't recovered.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.680
- We experiment with aerial drones

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.520
to see which one is gonna
give us the best information

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.340
at the best time while
we're flying in the storm.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.010
And so, you know, it's a trial and error.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.510
- [Narrator] For the 2023 season,

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.830
NOAA is also using Black
Swift S0 and Dragoon drones

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.590
with a new probe that can
measure vertical winds

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:20.190
allowing researchers to predict
how long a storm will last

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.260
at its highest intensity.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.020
As NOAA scientists
continue to chart the seas

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.090
in anticipation of hurricanes,

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:31.080
they see the information
provided by drones as vital.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.870
- These datasets, whether
it's in the ocean,

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.660
under the water, or even in the air,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.270
they're a key piece for us to understand

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.553
and forecast those storms.

